{"reference":"Judges 18","book":"Judges","chapter":18,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Dan takes Micah’s cult objects and his Levite, then establishes both at Dan after the tribe’s migration. The episode therefore traces a movement from a household’s religious property and patronage to a lasting tribal cult arrangement.","verse_refs":["Judges 18:1-31"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_018_e04763db2d7bb9f91fb1a12b","reader_path_judges_018_8e597b653cff8e2cb4a25533"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"inference"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions to Consider","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The statement that Israel had no king introduces a question about leadership in these closing narratives, which repeatedly connect that condition with people doing what seemed right to them. Even so, the statement alone does not settle whether every form of monarchy is endorsed; the book’s portrayal of Abimelech and the warnings in 1 Samuel must also remain in view.","verse_refs":["Judges 18:1"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_018_282a0854688916bd725685ac"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_3","text":"Archaeological comparisons should be used cautiously. The Bull Site is known as a small open cult place with a bronze bull figurine, but its users and deity cannot be securely identified. Likewise, occupation remains at Shiloh do not by themselves identify Micah’s shrine, the Danite installation, the house of God, or the festival mentioned elsewhere in Judges.","verse_refs":["Judges 18:1-31","Judges 18:30-31"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_018_064912aea55e6ea42a2187b4"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Within Judges","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Judges as a whole moves from incomplete possession and covenant indictment through recurring deliverance stories toward accounts of internal idolatry, atrocity, and civil war. This episode belongs to that later movement, where the conflict is increasingly within Israel itself.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_judges_018_b092a2e42693f840507eef12"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}